[TEXT]
MID: 12f0d31e9e43198819376eae71605c19
STATEMENT: >>>
[LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-DEPLOYMENT-LIFECYCLE-unified-cloudflare-management, 単一 Cloudflare Tunnel と製品公開分離] が管理経路と公開ゲートの契約を所有する。正式運用には統合実サービス受入を要する。

Provision a VM, enroll its first owner through the normal frontend and activate the certified Backend. The activation ends at ACTIVE. Complete the independent restore drill after configuring backups to establish operational acceptance. [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-INITIAL-DEPLOYMENT-FLOW, Initial deployment] owns the input and recovery contract. [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-DEPLOYMENT-LIFECYCLE, Deployment lifecycle] owns route selection. The accepted replacement management path is GitHub Actions → Cloudflare Access Service Auth → Cloudflare Tunnel → pinned OpenSSH → Ansible → rootless Podman Quadlet.
<<<

[[SECTION]]
MID: 7289693834063c1a8a9a4c98c60bf951
UID: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-PRODUCTION-BOOTSTRAP-guided-initial-deployment
TITLE: Guided initial deployment

[TEXT]
MID: 70dea02104b62424998e7344b56480c5
STATEMENT: >>>
Use the [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-CODEXIACTL, initial deployment CLI] from a pinned OpenSSH session on the target Ubuntu 26.04 x86-64 VM. It prepares the inputs below and invokes the existing protected workflows. The individual steps in this runbook are available for diagnosis and independent recovery. Operator approval and owner enrollment remain mandatory; Environment reviewers and Prevent self-review are optional. Both deployment Environments must restrict deployment to branch main only.

On your trusted workstation, select a published immutable official CLI version from the [Codexia releases](https://github.com/yohn-jp/codexia/releases), including when deploying a fork. The download and attestation repository below is the **official CLI publisher**, not the deployment repository. Replace `vX.Y.Z` with that exact tag and use an up-to-date GitHub CLI to verify the release and the binary's provenance.

```sh
tag=vX.Y.Z
repo=yohn-jp/codexia
gh release verify "$tag" --repo "$repo"
gh release download "$tag" --repo "$repo" \
  --pattern codexiactl-linux-amd64 --pattern codexiactl-linux-amd64.sha256
sha256sum --check --strict codexiactl-linux-amd64.sha256
gh attestation verify ./codexiactl-linux-amd64 --repo "$repo" \
  --signer-workflow "$repo/.github/workflows/publish-codexiactl.yml" \
  --source-ref "refs/tags/$tag" --deny-self-hosted-runners
```

Copy the binary and checksum to the VM over the independently verified SSH connection. Run these commands from the receiving directory as the deployment administrator.

```sh
sha256sum --check --strict codexiactl-linux-amd64.sha256
chmod 0700 codexiactl-linux-amd64
./codexiactl-linux-amd64 version
./codexiactl-linux-amd64 doctor
./codexiactl-linux-amd64 init --plan
./codexiactl-linux-amd64 init
```

The release identifies its official source and version. The official publisher provisions the public Codexia GitHub App and binds its public Client ID to release builds using `CODEXIACTL_GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID`. On the official App installation page, choose **Only select repositories** and select the intended `codexia` repository. In the browser Device Flow, sign in as a user authorized to operate that repository. The CLI confirms one discovered repository or prompts you to choose among eligible repositories. Review the selected repository, protected `main`, VM and public origin before approving. The same steps apply to the publisher's repository and to forks. See the [App installation page](https://github.com/apps/codexiactl/installations/new) and the [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-CODEXIACTL-41-github公式-app-による認証配備対象の発見, GitHub authentication and target discovery contract].

Keep your existing verified SSH connection available and have an independently verified host key fingerprint ready to compare with the observed keys. The CLI uses Cloudflare’s official headless Device Flow, provisions the required limited credentials, and verifies the host connector from the independently pinned session. If optional Environment reviewers are configured, use the GitHub approval UI for the runs the CLI identifies. Use `doctor` to identify missing prerequisites or permissions and `status` for the latest verified lifecycle observation. After an interrupted invocation, run `init` again from the same VM and administrator account. Resolve reported state conflicts through the owning protected workflow or recovery runbook before retrying.

After the CLI confirms the normal frontend and the registration-only API and hands off the Owner Setup Grant, the owner opens the normal `/console/setup` page. Use the existing protected procedures below after owner registration.

Before changing protected GitHub Settings, arrange an exclusive administrative window for both Environments. Pause other administrators, integrations and automated credential writers for the window. The CLI acquires one create-only coordination marker in `codexia-bootstrap` for both Environments. GitHub's Secrets endpoint uses create-or-update semantics. Stop if exclusive administrative access cannot be established. Resume an interrupted write from the same VM and administrative account with the existing checkpoint. Reconcile an unmatched marker through the authorized GitHub maintenance operation.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
MID: 76010b8b4da141471f4b87820c23e269
UID: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-PRODUCTION-BOOTSTRAP-establish-deployment-access
TITLE: Establish deployment access

[TEXT]
MID: b0f8e76da892707a613c9c7dc5f25e48
STATEMENT: >>>
Use GitHub Environments restricted to branch main; required reviewers and Prevent self-review are optional. Explicit operator approvals remain mandatory. The protected runners reach the VM through the dedicated Access Service Auth hostname on the shared Tunnel. The independent bootstrap administrator must have noninteractive sudo. Obtain the VM's SSH host key through the provider console or an independently authenticated channel. Provision the administrator's public SSH key on that account. Provision a separate deployment identity for the rootless service. R4 derives its public half inside the runtime Environment, installs it on the target, and verifies a real runtime login against the same VM identity.

| Location | Inputs | Who supplies them |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [Production configuration](https://github.com/yohn-jp/codexia/blob/main/deploy/production.json) in Git | Public origin, management SSH hostname, Desktop callback path and scopes, Web/iOS/introspection client IDs, rootless UID/GID | Maintainers review changes in a PR. The build and controller read the same settings. |
| `codexia-bootstrap` | Variables `CODEXIA_BOOTSTRAP_HOST`, `CODEXIA_BOOTSTRAP_SSH_USER`, `CODEXIA_BOOTSTRAP_SSH_KNOWN_HOSTS`. Secrets `CODEXIA_BOOTSTRAP_SSH_PRIVATE_KEY`, `CODEXIA_SSH_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID`, `CODEXIA_SSH_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET`. | The CLI verifies the independent administrator and registers its separate key and bootstrap Service Token. |
| `codexia-production` | Deployment SSH key, host pin, management hostname and separately scoped SSH Service Token in [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-PUSH-DEPLOYMENT-protected-inputs, Push deployment] | Runtime operator. The separately protected private key stays in this Environment. |
| Bootstrap and production observers | Variable `CODEXIA_DEPLOYMENT_CLOUDFLARE_TARGET`, Secrets `CODEXIA_DEPLOYMENT_CLOUDFLARE_READ_TOKEN`, `CODEXIA_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID`, `CODEXIA_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET` | The target JSON carries canonical `repository`, numeric `repositoryId` and `ownerId`, the certified `origin`, and the observed `accountId`, `zoneId`, `tunnelId`, `authorPolicyId`, `ciPolicyId`, `sshHostname`, `sshPolicyId`, `tunnelTeamName` and `publicProtocolEnabled`. The normal frontend release uses its separate Worker publication credential and checks its account against this target. |
| VM host connector | Root-owned protected connector credential | The CLI obtains the credential for the observed Tunnel and supplies it to the host systemd service before R4. |
| `codexia-bootstrap`, R5 grant issuance | Variable `CODEXIA_OWNER_SETUP_AGE_RECIPIENT` | Owner provides an independently verified age public key for delivery of the registration grant. |
| `codexia-bootstrap` | Variables `CODEXIA_BOOTSTRAP_ENABLED` and `CODEXIA_DATABASE_INITIALIZATION_APPROVED` | Operator enables the requested operation. Database initialization requires its own approval and dispatch selection. Both gates default closed. |

The controller builds a single-host Ansible inventory in a private runner directory. R4 verifies the active host connector and installs the closed publication gate. On a fresh target the VM generates the PostgreSQL admin, application and auth-role passwords, Better Auth secret and OAuth introspection secret. On an existing target, the VM checks its existing identities and values. Missing credentials with existing data, a connector identity mismatch, a UID conflict or an unverified DB state require reconciliation before proceeding.

Before enabling these workflows on an existing installation, review the repository and owner IDs, the main source's origin and the live Cloudflare topology. Add the reviewed binding to the existing Cloudflare target Variable in both protected Environments. Leave the independently verified credentials in their protected locations. The workflow rejects the old unbound target and any value copied from another repository. Use a fresh lifecycle Preflight after reconciling the Variables.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
MID: 8f7ecb7df289d7bdd37e3035297cdb03
UID: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-PRODUCTION-BOOTSTRAP-read-only-lifecycle-preflight
TITLE: Read-only lifecycle preflight

[TEXT]
MID: 194ce061653fe1ad27dfefae5682032c
STATEMENT: >>>
1. Run [Production Bootstrap](https://github.com/yohn-jp/codexia/blob/main/.github/workflows/production-bootstrap.yml) with `mode=preflight` to check the pinned administrator login, sudo and generated inventory. This connection test takes no DB passwords, Tunnel token, recovery credentials or release identifiers.
2. Open [Production Access Preflight](https://github.com/yohn-jp/codexia/blob/main/.github/workflows/production-preflight.yml) on main. Select `mode=lifecycle`, an intent and the management identity. Select `bootstrap` for R4 and the two R5 owner operations, and `runtime` for initial frontend publication and R6. For R6 supply the approved UTC window via `window_start` and `window_end`. When observing an existing writer, the prior publisher reference can identify its installed certificate.
3. Review the candidate route, target, blockers, required approvals and window in the redacted plan. Preflight resolves the certified Backend and compatible Full frontend from CI evidence. A plan whose only blocker is approval can be reviewed. Missing evidence or unresolved source identity stops the operation.
4. Pass that successful Preflight's run ID as `preflight_run_id` to the owning workflow. The workflow retrieves attempt 1 from the same repository, main SHA and management identity, recertifies the candidate and repeats the live observations in the production queue. After state changes or a rerun, start a new Preflight dispatch and review its new ID.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
MID: 4d78e1bc54e322b975dc8fe809c8e496
UID: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-PRODUCTION-BOOTSTRAP-run-r4-and-r5
TITLE: Run R4 and R5

[TEXT]
MID: 6a430cdb64890e41124f88c7c8ada887
STATEMENT: >>>
| Operation | Workflow and inputs | Evidence to review |
| --- | --- | --- |
| R4 VM and DB | Production Bootstrap, `mode=bootstrap`, reviewed administrator Preflight ID and `BOOTSTRAP_CODEXIA`. Select `initialize_database` only for separately authorized initialization. | Pinned host, actual service-account SSH, approved DB identity and internally generated credentials. No backup destination, escrow, or recovery proof is required. |
| R5 initial frontend | After R4, select a new runtime Preflight for `intent=install`. Run the existing [Frontend release](https://github.com/yohn-jp/codexia/blob/main/.github/workflows/frontend-release.yml) with its ID and `PUBLISH_INITIAL_FRONTEND`. | Owner pending, ordinary writer absent and certified archive on the normal Worker. |
| R5 issue grant | Obtain a fresh administrator plan. Run Production Bootstrap with `mode=owner-setup`, the run ID and `OWNER_SETUP_CODEXIA`. | The setup-only API verifies the edge and client bindings. Deliver the [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-ANSIBLE-AUTH-RUNTIME-first-owner-through-the-browser, encrypted handoff] to the owner for Passkey registration at `/console/setup`. |
| R5 prepare activation | Observe the enrolled owner in a new administrator plan. Run `mode=prepare-activation` with that ID and `OWNER_SETUP_CODEXIA`. | Owner and clients verified, setup-only API stopped, publication gate closed, clean API slot. No initial backup or independent restore gate. |
| R6 first Backend | Obtain a fresh runtime Preflight for `intent=install` with a valid window. Use [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-PUSH-DEPLOYMENT-first-application-activation, protected Backend activation] with that run ID, `ACTIVATE_CODEXIA_BACKEND`, and the protected first-install, DB-use and push approvals. | Exact candidate, compatible deployed frontend, same prepared owner, verified live DB/Auth/ledger, readiness and external Console. First configure and verify ordinary backup/recovery before accepting routine production operation; then R8 is available. |
| R6 existing-owner adoption | For a clean API slot and **existing verified Better Auth owner**, request a fresh runtime Preflight with `intent=adopt` and an approved UTC window. Supply a matching independent preservation receipt through the existing protected Preflight authority; use the Backend activation workflow with `ADOPT_EXISTING_CODEXIA_BACKEND`. Enable only `CODEXIA_DATABASE_ADOPTION_APPROVED=true` and ordinary push deployment; both initial-install and database-initialization approvals must remain false. | Verify host, cluster/OID, Auth schema, owner Passkey, settings, secrets, preserved-data binding and compatible Frontend before activation. Do not create or re-register an owner, initialize an existing database or bypass the Tunnel publication fence. |
| After ACTIVE, provision backups | Prepare the [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-BACKUP-CONSOLE-OPERATIONS, protected VM policy and public recovery recipient], then run Production Bootstrap with `mode=provision-backups`. Leave the initialization and lifecycle approval inputs empty or disabled. | The existing `codexia-production` runtime identity installs the executor from the admitted Full image. The owner selects and verifies the destination in the Console. |
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
MID: 82d137661d9e9fd5c962b7a2cb2bf8ba
UID: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-PRODUCTION-BOOTSTRAP-publishing-the-normal-frontend-for-first-owner-enrollment
TITLE: Publishing the normal frontend for first-owner enrollment

[TEXT]
MID: 0ba5234eb6049eb1e890b8c75e440d6f
STATEMENT: >>>
The normal Full frontend contains the Desktop `/console/setup` route. Provision the canonical Worker and routes under the existing Cloudflare infrastructure approval, using `manage_frontend_worker=true` when absent and `frontend_route_enabled=true`. Keep the public OAuth/MCP bypass disabled until normal runtime acceptance. The protected R5 publication verifies the archive and canonical Access routes, checks the monotonic frontend identity and reconciles its immutable release record. A retry after an interrupted upload or record write requires fresh observation. The same Worker serves the owner after activation.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
MID: 5cab96bb5f200068d5deb705954afcb6
UID: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-PRODUCTION-BOOTSTRAP-interrupted-installations-and-recovery
TITLE: Interrupted installations and recovery

[TEXT]
MID: 20cd0e7d72bacd0621c95b5f6ffcc21f
STATEMENT: >>>
A positively absent logical DB can be initialized with install intent and explicit DB approval. The controller rechecks absence and the installation checkpoint under the host lock before touching DB-bound deployment state. A matching cluster ID, database OID, current ledger and certified image allow the documented Auth-only continuation. Restore intent routes to recovery. Existing owner identity, Passkey and registered clients must survive adoption or restoration.

If a VM already has intact credentials and is waiting for owner setup or first activation, obtain a fresh plan and continue the applicable operation. The fresh R6 path requires the existing installation intent to match the live PostgreSQL cluster, database OID and certified Backend. Send other clean-slot activations to the protected adoption or recovery procedure. Verify the existing values and identities; do not export, escrow, regenerate, or require a separate proof of their recovery as a fresh-install condition. For restore intent, stop and follow R2 and [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-BACKUP-通常運用のバックアップと災害復旧, Backup].

If the grant expired or the encrypted handoff was lost, follow the [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-ANSIBLE-AUTH-RUNTIME-first-owner-through-the-browser, separately approved owner reissue procedure]. A prepared owner does not require reissue. After R6, the owner opens `/console/backups` and configures the ordinary backup destination; the recovery operator independently verifies restore before routine production acceptance. Keep S3 and restore credentials in the ordinary backup and independent recovery procedures.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]
